<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">ArcelorMittal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2006">2006</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">ArcelorMittal is a multinational <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> and mining company formed in 2006 when Mittal Steel took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcelor">Arcelor</a>. Headquartered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, it is one of the largest steel producers in the world, with plants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. It continues the industrial tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbed">ARBED</a>, long the dominant firm in the Luxembourg economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arcelormittal.jpg" alt="ArcelorMittal" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meffo · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ArcelorMittalLuxemburg.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>ArcelorMittal has its headquarters in Luxembourg City and is a multinational <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> manufacturer with operations in iron ore and coke mining and in scrap recycling. It was created when Mittal Steel Company acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcelor">Arcelor</a>, in a purchase worth 33 billion dollars. The merger was completed after Mittal Steel raised its bid and the Mittal family agreed to give up a controlling stake and signed a standstill agreement not to take a controlling interest without the approval of independent directors. The resulting firm produced about 10 per cent of the world's steel and was then the largest steel company in the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 2007 the company bought Sicartsa, which runs a plant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, from Grupo Villacero for 1.44 billion dollars and 90 per cent of the Chinese steel wire company Rongcheng Chengshan Steelcord. It raised its stake in China Oriental Group to over 73 per cent, becoming the first foreign company to control a steel mill in China. To satisfy the conditions for regulatory approval of the merger it sold the Bethlehem Steel plant at Lackawanna, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, and the Sparrows Point Mill to Severstal in December 2008. In the same month, in the Great Recession, it closed its steel mill at Hennepin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>.</p><p>In January 2011 the company spun off its stainless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> division as Aperam. In 2012, because of overcapacity and weaker demand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, it idled 9 of its 25 blast furnaces and shut down two furnaces at Florange, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but revived them two months later after agreeing a substantial investment with the French government. In 2013 it sold a 15 per cent interest in its Labrador Trough iron ore assets to Posco and China Steel for 1.1 billion dollars.</p><p>In 2014 an International Chamber of Commerce court awarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> 150 million dollars and allowed it to cancel a 2.2 billion dollar contract to develop an iron ore deposit and a 750 km railway after the company stalled on construction. A consortium led by the company bought Ilva, owner of the Taranto steelworks in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, in November 2018 for 1.8 billion euros.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The company has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>-making operations in 14 countries and iron ore mining in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, Liberia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. It is vertically integrated, producing much of its own iron ore and coke. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a> fined it 45.7 million euros in April 2011, the largest of the fines on 17 producers, for taking part in a steel cartel between 1984 and 2002, and in 2016 the South African Competition Commission fined it for price fixing between 1984 and 2002.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “ArcelorMittal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>ARBED merged into Arcelor in 2002, which became <b>ArcelorMittal</b> in 2006 with its headquarters in Luxembourg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
